Title: "Von Neumann Numerals"
Id: "b21aeb46-c87a-41ea-8a30-8bf598aee2f6"


Text: "John von Neumann provided a canonical set-theoretic realization of the
       natural numbers. In this construction, zero is the empty set $\emptyset$,
       and each successive number is the set of all numbers preceding it:
       $0 = \emptyset$, $1 = \{0\} = \{\emptyset\}$, $2 = \{0, 1\}$, and in
       general $S(n) = n \cup \{n\}$. This elegant encoding makes the membership
       relation $\in$ coincide with the strict order $<$ on the naturals."
Id: "c7eca060-3bf4-4bc8-a372-210f25fb71cc"


SectionTitle: "Inductive Sets and the Minimal Inductive Set"
Id: "ce4cc152-49d8-4c94-a23e-7c5d615e1d11"


[\(inductive)::set]
Refines: I
satisfies:
. \empty.set "in"? I
. forAll: X is \set
  where: X "in"? I
  then: \set.successor:of{X} "in"? I
Documented:
. adjective: "inductive"
. description: "An inductive set is a set containing the empty set and closed
                under the set successor operation $X \mapsto X \cup \{X\}$."
Id: "68ba43e9-1329-4103-819e-16e20e8e7c9f"


Text: "The set $\omega$ of all Von Neumann numerals is the intersection of all
       inductive sets, existing by virtue of the Axiom of Infinity."
Id: "9cd13132-60e1-4e1a-9e3c-525d1b2218e5"


[\von.neumann.omega]
Defines: omega is \set
expresses:
. let:
  . (.A is \(inductive)::set.)[:1:]
  . X is \set
  then:
  . have: X "in"? omega
    iff:
    . X "in"? A
    . forAll: I is \(inductive)::set
      then: X "in"? I
Enables:
. capability: X_ "in" omega :-> X_ is \set
Documented:
. called: "Von Neumann Numerals"
. written: "\omega"
. description: "The set $\omega$ of all Von Neumann numerals, the smallest
                inductive set."
Justification:
. [1]
  have: A is \(inductive)::set
  by: \axiom.of.infinity
Id: "c13f4641-0ed5-4ad7-b309-8ec13b4c6b77"


SectionTitle: "Successor and Canonical Realization"
Id: "54bc28ae-1234-4b56-8a90-33cd78912ef0"


[\von.neumann.S(n_)]
Defines: S(n_) is \function:on{\von.neumann.omega}:to{\von.neumann.omega}
expresses: S(n_) := \set.successor:of{n_}
Documented:
. written: "S(n_?)"
. called: "Von Neumann Successor"
. description: "The Von Neumann successor function $S(n) = n \cup \{n\}$ on
                $\omega$."
Id: "431ccff0-c289-46a7-b739-211c8ae805b4"


[\von.neumann.naturals]
Realizes: Nb ::= (N, 0, S(n_)) := \naturals
specifies:
. N := \von.neumann.omega
. 0 := \empty.set
. S(n_) := \von.neumann.S(n_)
Enables:
. capability: x_ "in" Nb :<->: x_ "in" N
Documented:
. called: "Von Neumann Naturals"
. description: "The concrete realization of the Peano natural numbers as the Von
                Neumann ordinal structure $(\omega, \emptyset, S)$."
Id: "23196d3d-7a1f-44a8-899c-3c39d65bb331"


[\to.von.neumann.natural{n}]
Defines: m "in" \von.neumann.naturals
when: n is \natural
expresses:
. piecewise:
  if: n = 0
  then: m = \empty.set
  else:
  . let: k is \natural
    where: n = \naturals..S(k)
    then: m = \set.successor:of{\to.von.neumann.natural{k}}
Documented:
. called: "Von Neumann Numeral Equivalent of $n?$"
. written: "[n?]"
. description: "The canonical map from abstract Peano naturals to their Von
                Neumann set-theoretic numeral representatives."
Id: "3aa2a394-dea8-48d6-8b73-6ad032408fe2"
